@anon.879c592214345ca999bafaa324406990.835 = private unnamed_addr constant [999 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for code that branches on `sys.version_info` comparisons where\0Abranches corresponding to older Python versions come before branches\0Acorresponding to newer Python versions.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AAs a convention, branches that correspond to newer Python versions should\0Acome first. This makes it easier to understand the desired behavior, which\0Atypically corresponds to the latest Python versions.\0A\0AThis rule enforces the convention by checking for `if` tests that compare\0A`sys.version_info` with `<` rather than `>=`.\0A\0ABy default, this rule only applies to stub files.\0AIn [preview], it will also flag this anti-pattern in non-stub files.\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```pyi\0Aimport sys\0A\0Aif sys.version_info < (3, 10):\0A    def read_data(x, *, preserve_order=True): ...\0A\0Aelse:\0A    def read_data(x): ...\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A\0A```pyi\0Aif sys.version_info >= (3, 10):\0A    def read_data(x): ...\0A\0Aelse:\0A    def read_data(x, *, preserve_order=True): ...\0A```\0A\0A[preview]: https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/preview/\0A", align 1
@anon.879c592214345ca999bafaa324406990.836 = private unnamed_addr constant [93 x i8] c"Put branches for newer Python versions first when branching on `sys.version_info` comparisons", align 1
