@anon.879c592214345ca999bafaa324406990.872 = private unnamed_addr constant [3471 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for the use of `glob.glob()` and `glob.iglob()`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A`pathlib` offers a high-level API for path manipulation, as compared to\0Athe lower-level API offered by `os` and `glob`.\0A\0AWhen possible, using `Path` object methods such as `Path.glob()` can\0Aimprove readability over their low-level counterparts (e.g.,\0A`glob.glob()`).\0A\0ANote that `glob.glob()` and `Path.glob()` are not exact equivalents:\0A\0A|                   | `glob`-module functions                                                                                                                              | `Path.glob()`                                                                                                                                |\0A|-------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\0A| Hidden files      | Hidden files are excluded by default. On Python 3.11+, the `include_hidden` keyword can be used to include hidden directories.                       | Includes hidden files by default.                                                                                                            |\0A| Eagerness         | `glob.iglob()` returns a lazy iterator. Under the hood, `glob.glob()` simply converts the iterator to a list.                                        | `Path.glob()` returns a lazy iterator.                                                                                                       |\0A| Working directory | `glob.glob()` and `glob.iglob()` take a `root_dir` keyword to set the current working directory.                                                     | `Path.rglob()` can be used to return the relative path.                                                                                      |\0A| Globstar (`**`)   | The `recursive` flag must be set to `True` for the `**` pattern to match any files and zero or more directories, subdirectories, and symbolic links. | The `**` pattern in `Path.glob()` means \22this directory and all subdirectories, recursively\22. In other words, it enables recursive globbing. |\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport glob\0Aimport os\0A\0Aglob.glob(os.path.join(\22my_path\22, \22requirements*.txt\22))\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afrom pathlib import Path\0A\0APath(\22my_path\22).glob(\22requirements*.txt\22)\0A```\0A\0A## Known issues\0AWhile using `pathlib` can improve the readability and type safety of your code,\0Ait can be less performant than the lower-level alternatives that work directly with strings,\0Aespecially on older versions of Python.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `Path.glob`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/pathlib.html#pathlib.Path.glob)\0A- [Python documentation: `Path.rglob`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/pathlib.html#pathlib.Path.rglob)\0A- [Python documentation: `glob.glob`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/glob.html#glob.glob)\0A- [Python documentation: `glob.iglob`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/glob.html#glob.iglob)\0A- [PEP 428 \E2\80\93 The pathlib module \E2\80\93 object-oriented filesystem paths](https://peps.python.org/pep-0428/)\0A- [Why you should be using pathlib](https://treyhunner.com/2018/12/why-you-should-be-using-pathlib/)\0A- [No really, pathlib is great](https://treyhunner.com/2019/01/no-really-pathlib-is-great/)\0A", align 1
