@anon.879c592214345ca999bafaa324406990.930 = private unnamed_addr constant [933 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for assignment of `self` and `cls` in instance and class methods respectively.\0A\0AThis check also applies to `__new__` even though this is technically\0Aa static method.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AThe identifiers `self` and `cls` are conventional in Python for the first parameter of instance\0Amethods and class methods, respectively. Assigning new values to these variables can be\0Aconfusing for others reading your code; using a different variable name can lead to clearer\0Acode.\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```python\0Aclass Version:\0A    def add(self, other):\0A        self = self + other\0A        return self\0A\0A    @classmethod\0A    def superclass(cls):\0A        cls = cls.__mro__[-1]\0A        return cls\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aclass Version:\0A    def add(self, other):\0A        new_version = self + other\0A        return new_version\0A\0A    @classmethod\0A    def superclass(cls):\0A        supercls = cls.__mro__[-1]\0A        return supercls\0A```\0A", align 1
