@anon.879c592214345ca999bafaa324406990.984 = private unnamed_addr constant [932 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for long exception messages that are not defined in the exception\0Aclass itself.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ABy formatting an exception message at the `raise` site, the exception class\0Abecomes less reusable, and may now raise inconsistent messages depending on\0Awhere it is raised.\0A\0AIf the exception message is instead defined within the exception class, it\0Awill be consistent across all `raise` invocations.\0A\0AThis rule is not enforced for some built-in exceptions that are commonly\0Araised with a message and would be unusual to subclass, such as\0A`NotImplementedError`.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aclass CantBeNegative(Exception):\0A    pass\0A\0A\0Adef foo(x):\0A    if x < 0:\0A        raise CantBeNegative(f\22{x} is negative\22)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aclass CantBeNegative(Exception):\0A    def __init__(self, number):\0A        super().__init__(f\22{number} is negative\22)\0A\0A\0Adef foo(x):\0A    if x < 0:\0A        raise CantBeNegative(x)\0A```\0A", align 1
